**Why 36-month loans from 2012 to 2015.** The original brief suggested 2015-2018. At the data snapshot, {{ m.censoring.share_36m_2016 | pct(0) }} of 36-month loans issued in 2016 and {{ m.censoring.share_36m_2017 | pct(0) }} of those issued in 2017 were still running. Keeping only their finished loans would over-sample early defaults and early payoffs and bias the default rate. For 36-month loans issued 2012-2015, only {{ m.censoring.scope_non_terminal_share | pct(2) }} are unresolved. 60-month loans are left out ({{ m.censoring.share_60m_2015 | pct(0) }} of the 2015 vintage still running) and listed as a next step.

![Share of loans still running by vintage](reports/figures/censoring_by_vintage.png)

**Known quirks.** Every loan in the file was approved by Lending Club, so the data says nothing about rejected applicants. FICO at application starts at {{ d.fico_min | num }} (Lending Club's floor). Many bureau fields (for example `tot_cur_bal`, `mort_acc`) only start in mid-2012, so they are empty for the first months of training; LightGBM and the scorecard's missing bin handle this. The {{ d.raw_rows | compact(2) }}-row CSV is never loaded into pandas: DuckDB reads only the columns each query needs.

## 3. Approach

1. **Target.** Default = `Charged Off` or `Default`; good = `Fully Paid`. Loans still current, in grace or late at the snapshot are excluded and counted per vintage (`reports/tables/scope_flow.csv`).
2. **Out-of-time split.** Train on 2012-2014, tune and recalibrate on January-June 2015, test once on July-December 2015. A random split would let the model learn from loans issued after the ones it is scored on.
3. **Leakage audit.** Every one of the {{ au.n_columns }} raw columns has a documented decision in [`docs/feature_audit.csv`](docs/feature_audit.csv): available at application or not, category, keep or drop, and why. {{ au.n_candidate_features }} application-time features remain. Lending Club's grade, sub-grade, interest rate and instalment are excluded as features: they are the incumbent model's output, so they serve as the benchmark and in the P&L.
4. **Three rankers.** (a) Lending Club sub-grade, the incumbent. (b) A logistic scorecard on weight-of-evidence bins (optbinning, monotonic bins, IV filter, correlation filter, coefficient sign check, {{ md.scaling.base_points }} points at {{ md.scaling.base_odds }}:1 odds, {{ md.scaling.pdo }} points to double the odds). (c) LightGBM with early stopping on validation and a small grid. All three are recalibrated on validation (Platt scaling) because 2015 defaulted more than 2012-2014.
5. **Explanations.** SHAP values on LightGBM, turned into plain-English reason codes for each applicant.
6. **Money, not AUC, picks the cutoff.** Expected profit per loan = principal x [(1 - PD) x G - PD x L - costs], with G and L measured on the training window. The cutoff is chosen on validation and judged on the test window with the cash each loan actually returned.

## 4. Findings

### 4.1 The same grade meant more risk at a lower price in 2015

| Grade | Default rate 2013 | Default rate 2015 | Avg. rate 2013 | Avg. rate 2015 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for g in ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"] %}
| {{ g }} | {{ v.by_grade[g].dr_2013 | pct }} | {{ v.by_grade[g].dr_2015 | pct }} | {{ v.by_grade[g].rate_2013 | pct }} | {{ v.by_grade[g].rate_2015 | pct }} |
{% endfor %}

Default rates rose inside every grade while interest rates fell in grades A to E. Splitting the {{ dec.change | pp }} rise between 2013 and 2015: the grade mix contributed {{ dec.mix_effect | pp }} (it shifted toward safer grades) and higher default rates within grades contributed {{ dec.rate_effect | pp }}. By quarter, the default rate bottomed at {{ v.min_quarter_rate | pct }} ({{ v.min_quarter }}) and peaked at {{ v.max_quarter_rate | pct }} ({{ v.max_quarter }}). This is an association across vintages, not a causal estimate: Lending Club's pricing, marketing and the credit cycle all changed at once.

![Default rate by issue quarter](reports/figures/default_rate_by_quarter.png)

### 4.2 Post-origination fields look like magic and are useless at decision time

A single-column screen on the training window ranks every raw field by how well it separates defaults on its own. The {{ au.n_flagged }} columns above {{ au.flag_threshold | dec }} are all recorded after the loan is issued (strongest: `{{ au.max_post_column }}` at {{ au.max_post_auc | dec }}). The best genuine application field, FICO, reaches {{ au.max_app_auc | dec }}; Lending Club's sub-grade {{ au.subgrade_auc_train | dec }}. Retraining the final LightGBM with one leaked field shows the damage: test AUC goes from {{ lk.baseline_auc | dec(3) }} to {{ lk.auc_with_last_fico | dec(3) }} with the refreshed FICO score and to {{ lk.auc_with_total_rec_prncp | dec(4) }} with principal repaid to date.

![Single-field AUC screen](reports/figures/leakage_auc_screen.png)

Other drops, all justified in the audit file: identifiers and free text (`emp_title` has very high cardinality and can proxy for protected characteristics), `zip_code` and `addr_state` (geography can proxy for race and ethnicity; ECOA / Regulation B in the US, and the EU AI Act classes creditworthiness scoring as high-risk), {{ au.by_category.late_2015_field }} bureau fields that only exist from late 2015, and {{ au.by_category.joint_applicant }} joint-applicant fields.

