### 4.5 Price does not cover risk at the top of the book

With base assumptions a loan breaks even at PD = (G - costs) / (G + L): {{ a.breakeven_pd["<8%"] | pct(0) }} for loans priced under 8%, {{ a.breakeven_pd["20%+"] | pct(0) }} for loans priced at 20% or more. On the test window, loans priced at 20% or more defaulted {{ p.by_band["20%+"].default_rate | pct }} of the time, for a realized profit of {{ p.by_band["20%+"].realized_profit | usd }}. Ranking applicants by expected margin (which rewards high prices) instead of by PD changed realized profit by {{ p.price_aware.gap_vs_pd_cutoff | susd }} at the same approval rate, so the recommended rule is a plain PD cutoff.

![Break-even PD by rate band](reports/figures/breakeven_by_band.png)

## 5. Recommendation and expected impact

**Decline applications with a LightGBM PD above {{ rec.pd_cutoff | pct }}** (the "balanced" option). The cutoff maximises expected profit on January-June 2015 subject to approving at least {{ rec.min_volume | pct(0) }} of applicants, the committee's volume constraint. It was then applied once to the July-December 2015 loans:

| Test window, Jul-Dec 2015 | Approve all (today) | Decline grades D-G | Conservative | **Balanced (recommended)** | Growth |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| PD cutoff | none | n/a | {{ cons.pd_cutoff | pct }} | **{{ rec.pd_cutoff | pct }}** | {{ grow.pd_cutoff | pct }} |
| Approval rate | {{ sq.approval_rate | pct }} | {{ dg.grade_rule.approval_rate | pct }} | {{ cons.approval_rate | pct }} | **{{ rec.approval_rate | pct }}** | {{ grow.approval_rate | pct }} |
| Dollar volume kept | {{ sq.volume_share | pct }} | {{ dg.grade_rule.volume_share | pct }} | {{ cons.volume_share | pct }} | **{{ rec.volume_share | pct }}** | {{ grow.volume_share | pct }} |
| Default rate, approved | {{ sq.default_rate | pct }} | {{ dg.grade_rule.default_rate | pct }} | {{ cons.default_rate | pct }} | **{{ rec.default_rate | pct }}** | {{ grow.default_rate | pct }} |
| Net credit loss | {{ sq.net_credit_loss | usd }} | {{ dg.grade_rule.net_credit_loss | usd }} | {{ cons.net_credit_loss | usd }} | **{{ rec.net_credit_loss | usd }}** | {{ grow.net_credit_loss | usd }} |
| Realized profit (half-year) | {{ sq.realized_profit | usd }} | {{ dg.grade_rule.realized_profit | usd }} | {{ cons.realized_profit | usd }} | **{{ rec.realized_profit | usd }}** | {{ grow.realized_profit | usd }} |

At the same approval rate as the "decline grades D-G" rule ({{ dg.grade_rule.approval_rate | pct }}), a LightGBM cutoff earns {{ dg.lgbm_same_rate.realized_profit | usd }} against {{ dg.grade_rule.realized_profit | usd }}, with a default rate of {{ dg.lgbm_same_rate.default_rate | pct }} against {{ dg.grade_rule.default_rate | pct }}. The scorecard at the balanced approval rate adds {{ p.scorecard_balanced.profit_change | susd }} against LightGBM's {{ rec.profit_change | susd }}: the price of the simpler model.

**What the recommended cutoff declines:** {{ rec.declined_n | num }} applicants, of whom {{ rec.declined_default_rate | pct }} defaulted. It gives up {{ rec.good_profit_forgone | usd }} of profit on {{ rec.good_loans_declined | num }} good loans and avoids larger losses on the rest, for a net realized gain of {{ rec.profit_change | susd }} in six months.

**Annual view.** The test window covers six months, so annual figures double it: a book of {{ p.annualisation.annual_loans | num }} loans and {{ p.annualisation.annual_amount | usd }} a year. Under base assumptions that is {{ p.annual.profit_change | susd }} profit, {{ p.annual.loss_avoided | usd }} of net credit losses and {{ p.annual.defaults_avoided | num }} defaults avoided, for {{ p.annual.volume_lost | usd }} less lending.

**Assumptions (G and L measured on 2012-2014 loans; costs are editable in the simulator):**

| Assumption | Low (favourable) | Base | High (adverse) | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| G: net interest earned per $ on loans that pay off, 11-14% band | {{ a.G_high["11-14%"] | pct }} | {{ a.G["11-14%"] | pct }} | {{ a.G_low["11-14%"] | pct }} | Best / pooled / worst training year |
| L: net loss per $ on a defaulted loan | {{ a.L_low | pct }} | {{ a.L | pct }} | {{ a.L_high | pct }} | Best / pooled / worst training year |
| Cost of funds, annual | {{ a.cost_of_funds.low | pct }} | {{ a.cost_of_funds.base | pct }} | {{ a.cost_of_funds.high | pct }} | Assumption |
| Principal-years outstanding per $ lent | {{ a.principal_years | dec }} | {{ a.principal_years | dec }} | {{ a.principal_years | dec }} | Interest received / rate, training |
| Operating cost, share of principal | {{ a.opex.low | pct }} | {{ a.opex.base | pct }} | {{ a.opex.high | pct }} | Assumption |
| **Annual profit gain of the balanced cutoff (realized cash)** | **{{ p.scenarios.low.annual_realized_profit_change | susd }}** | **{{ p.scenarios.base.annual_realized_profit_change | susd }}** | **{{ p.scenarios.high.annual_realized_profit_change | susd }}** | Test window x 2 |
| Annual profit gain (expected, PD x assumptions) | {{ p.scenarios.low.annual_expected_profit_change | susd }} | {{ p.scenarios.base.annual_expected_profit_change | susd }} | {{ p.scenarios.high.annual_expected_profit_change | susd }} | Test window x 2 |

Realized profit uses each loan's actual cash, so only the cost assumptions move it; expected profit also depends on G and L. The cash fields were checked before use: `total_pymnt` equals principal + interest + late fees + recoveries to within {{ p.cash.identity_max_abs_residual | dec }} dollars on every loan, and defaulted loans repaid {{ p.cash.bad_principal_repaid_share | pct }} of principal before charge-off. No time value of money is applied.

**Expected vs realized.** Expected profit overstates realized profit by {{ p.expected_vs_realized_gap | pct }} of the test book. The main reason: 2015 defaults lost {{ p.drift.test.L | pct }} of principal against {{ a.L | pct }} in training. Read the expected numbers as optimistic, and the realized ones as what the policy would have earned on these loans.

