## 6. Limitations and what I would do next

- **Accepted loans only.** Applicants Lending Club rejected never received a loan, so their outcomes do not exist and there is no reject inference. The recommended policy can only tighten; approving segments Lending Club declined would need a test on live traffic.
- **Selected population.** FICO starts at {{ d.fico_min | num }} and every borrower passed Lending Club's own filters. Results do not transfer to thin-file or subprime applicants.
- **Pricing held fixed.** The analysis takes Lending Club's prices as given. The 20%+ band loses money at observed default rates; re-pricing it is a separate decision with its own demand effects.
- **One credit cycle.** 2012-2015 was a benign period in the US. Losses on 2015 defaults already ran above the training calibration ({{ p.drift.test.L | pct }} vs {{ a.L | pct }}).
- **Marketplace vs balance sheet.** Lending Club sold loans to investors; Northvale is framed as a lender funding its own book, so cost of funds and operating costs are assumptions, not data.
- **Modest discrimination gain.** LightGBM adds {{ md.incremental.auc_lift_lgbm_vs_grade | dec(3) }} AUC over the sub-grade. Most of the value comes from using a calibrated PD and an explicit profit rule, not from model complexity.
- **Fairness not yet tested.** Protected attributes are not in the data, so approval and error rates by group cannot be measured directly. A fair-lending review (for example with BISG proxies) would come before any deployment.
- **No time value of money** in the P&L; a loan that defaults late is treated like one that defaults early.

**Next:** a champion-challenger trial on live applications; monthly monitoring of score PSI (alert at {{ m.monitoring.psi_alert | dec }}; today {{ m.monitoring.score_psi_lgbm | dec(3) }}), vintage default curves and manual override rates; 60-month loans once enough vintages resolve; monotonic constraints in LightGBM so its shape can be signed off like a scorecard.

## 7. How to reproduce

Requirements: macOS or Linux, Python 3.11, Node 18+ (deck only), LibreOffice (`soffice`, PDF export), about 2 GB of free disk. On macOS, LightGBM needs OpenMP: `brew install libomp`.

```bash
make setup   # .venv, pinned requirements, npm ci for the deck builder
make all     # data, build, analysis, notebooks, docs, deck, test
```

`make data` downloads the Kaggle bundle (about 1.3 GB, no login needed), extracts only the accepted-loans file and checks its SHA-256. A full `make setup && make all` from a fresh clone, with the raw file already in `data/raw/`, takes about 10 minutes on an Apple-silicon laptop. Seeds are fixed (`SEED = 42`); LightGBM runs with 4 threads in deterministic mode. Other targets: `make app` (simulator), `make deck-qa` (slide images), `make clean`.

